"For Annette, Cicely, Kim, Tony, and Josephine (https://files.eportfolios.macaulay.cuny.edu/wp-content/uploads/sites/6757/2019/10/16221820/Nathan-JURGENSEN-The-Social-Photo-excerpt.pdf)\nciteturn128search12 [wordlim: 200] First published by Verso 2019 ... Title: The social photo : on photography and social media / Nathan Jurgenson.\nFor Annette, Cicely, Kim, Tony, and Josephine\n\nFirst published by Verso 2019  \n© Nathan Jurgenson 2019\n\nAll rights reserved\n\nThe moral rights of the authors have been asserted\n\n1 3 5 7 9 10 8 6 4 2\n\nVerso  \nUK: 6 Meard Street, London W1F OEG  \nUS: 20 Jay Street, Suite 1010, Brooklyn, NY 11201  \nversobooks.com\n\nVerso is the imprint of New Left Books\n\nISBN-13: 978-1-78873-091-4  \nISBN-13: 978-1-78663-545-7 (UK EBK)  \nISBN-13: 978-1-78663-546-4 (US EBK)\n\nBritish Library Cataloguing in Publication Data  \nA catalogue record for this book is available from the British Library\n\nLibrary of Congress Cataloging-in-Publication Data\n\nNames: Jurgenson, Nathan, author.  \nTitle: The social photo : on photography and social media / Nathan Jurgenson.  \nDescription: New York : Verso Books, 2019. | Includes bibliographical references.  \nIdentifiers: LCCN 2018016700 | ISBN 9781788730914 | ISBN 9781786635457 (UK EBK) | ISBN 9781786635464 (US EBK)  \nSubjects: LCSH: Photography—Philosophy. | Photography—Social aspects. | Vernacular photography. | Computer file sharing. | Online social networks.  \nClassification: LCC TR183 .J87 2019 | DDC 770—dc23  \nLC record available at https://lccn.loc.gov/2018016700\n\nTypeset in Sabon by MJ & N Gavan, Truro, Cornwall  \nPrinted and bound by CPI Group (UK) Ltd, Croydon, CR0 4YY--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNathan Jurgenson (https://www.tridentmediagroup.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Frankfurt-Guide-2019-FOR-PRINTER-1.pdf)\nciteturn128search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.0 years ago; - Verso ... In THE SOCIAL PHOTO, social theorist Nathan Jurgenson develops bold new ways of understanding the transformations wrought by these image-making and sharing technologies and the cultural objects they have ushered in: the selfie, the faux-vintage photo, the self-destructing image, the food photo. ... Book cover image on the right showing the title “The Social Photo: On Photography and Social Media” with a large illustrated eye over the center and the author name “Nathan Jurgenson” at the bottom.\nNathan Jurgenson\n\n# THE SOCIAL PHOTO:\n*On Photography and Social Media*\n\n<visual_element id=\"e1\">\nLeft boxed notice with stacked text:\n- Verso\n- April 2019\n- Edited Manuscript Available\n- Trident: Translation\n- Dan Strone / DV\n</visual_element>\n\nA set of bold theoretical reflections on how the social photo has remade our world\n\nWith the rise of the smartphone and social media, cameras have become ubiquitous, infiltrating nearly every aspect of life. The glowing camera screen is the lens by which many of us understand and communicate our experience. But our thinking about photography has been slow to catch up; this major fixture of everyday life is still often treated in the terms of art or journalism.\n\nIn THE SOCIAL PHOTO, social theorist Nathan Jurgenson develops bold new ways of understanding the transformations wrought by these image-making and sharing technologies and the cultural objects they have ushered in: the selfie, the faux-vintage photo, the self-destructing image, the food photo. Jurgenson shows how these devices and platforms have remade the world and our understanding of ourselves within it.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAPRIL\n\nThe Social Photo  \nOn Photography and Socia (https://versobooks-prod.s3.amazonaws.com/static_files/f78f549bcd76da9f9838d624569a8c34/FINAL_FILES_%28LO-RES%29_Verso_Catalogue_%28Spring_2019%29.pdf)\nciteturn128search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.1 years ago; Crawled: 8.1 years ago; In *The Social Photo*, social theorist Nathan Jurgenson develops bold new ways of understanding the transformations wrought by these image-making and sharing technologies and the cultural objects they have ushered in: the selfie, the faux-vintage photo, the self-destructing image, the food photo.\nAPRIL\n\nThe Social Photo  \nOn Photography and Social Media  \nNathan Jurgenson\n\nA set of bold theoretical reflections on how the social photo has remade our world\n\nWith the rise of the smart phone and social media, cameras have become ubiquitous, infiltrating nearly every aspect of social life. The glowing camera screen is the lens by which many of us apprehend and communicate our experience. But our thinking about photography has been slow to catch up; this major fixture of everyday life is still often treated in the terms of art or journalism.\n\nIn *The Social Photo*, social theorist Nathan Jurgenson develops bold new ways of understanding the transformations wrought by these image-making and sharing technologies and the cultural objects they have ushered in: the selfie, the faux-vintage photo, the self-destructing image, the food photo. Jurgenson shows how these devices and platforms have remade the world and our understanding of ourselves within it.\n\nNathan Jurgenson is a social media theorist. He is co-founder and Co-Chair of the annual Theorizing the Web conference, founder and Editor-in-Chief of *Real Life* magazine, Editor Emeritus at *New Inquiry*, and a sociologist at Snap Inc. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBook Review (https://journals.ncert.gov.in/IJET/article/download/433/171/751)\nciteturn128search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.0 years ago; The Social Photo: On Photography and Social Media ... By: Jurgenson, Nathan, Published by Verso, 2019\nBook Review\nThe Social Photo: On Photography and Social Media \n(ISBN-13:978-1-78873-091-4) \nBy: Jurgenson, Nathan, Published by Verso, 2019\nMahashweta Bhattacharya\nResearch Scholar, Centre for Media Studies, Jawaharlal Nehru University (JNU),\nNew Delhi, Email: mahashweta96@gmail.com\nenquiry.\nThe book is divided into two quite hefty \nchapters, each of which runs in the form \nof a slightly discomforting Twitter-speak \nseries of threads woven together into a \nvery long essay. It is followed by a tiny \nessay for a third chapter, the existence \nof which is questionable.\nThe practice of photography has always \ninvited adequate probing and queries \nfrom such varied fields of scholarship \nas art history, visual culture and media \nstudies which have each viewed the \nsubject through its own unique lens \nand thus found something the others \noverlooked. \nLikewise, \nsocial \nmedia \nas an emergent phenomenon of any \nand every society has not escaped the \npiercing probosces of social science \ntheorists. \nThe first chapter, titled Documentary \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe social photo : on photography and social media | CiNii Research (https://cir.nii.ac.jp/crid/1970867909858702470)\nciteturn128search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.8 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # The social photo : on photography and social media ...   * Jurgenson, Nathan ...   * Verso ...   * 2019\n\n# The social photo : on photography and social media\n\nWeb Site CiNii 所蔵館 7館\n\n  * Jurgenson, Nathan\n    \n    \n\n  * 資料情報\n  * 所蔵館 (7館)\n\n## 書誌事項\n\nタイトル\n    \"The social photo : on photography and social media\"\n\n責任表示\n    Nathan Jurgenson\n\n出版者\n    \n\n  * Verso\n  *   * 出版年月\n    \n\n  * 2019\n\n書籍サイズ\n    22 cm\n\n## この図書・雑誌をさがす\n\n  * NDL SEARCH\n  * カーリル\n  * WorldCat\n\n## 詳細情報 詳細情報について\n\n  * CRID\n    1970867909858702470\n  * NII書誌ID\n    BB28617824\n  * ISBN\n    9781788730914\n  * LCCN\n    2018016700\n  * Web Site\n    https://lccn.loc.gov/2018016700\n  * 本文言語コード\n    en\n  * 出版国コード\n    uk\n  * タイトル言語コード\n    en\n  * 出版地\n    \n    * London\n  * 分類\n    \n    * LCC: TR183\